  "body": "STEP import now reads the file's declared length unit and converts to millimetres. A part saved in metres or inches imports at its true size, instead of having its numbers read as millimetres.\n\n### What was wrong (`main` 8b0d784)\n\n- `geometry/import/step.rs` tessellated the shells and kept every coordinate as millimetres. Nothing in `crates/` read a STEP `LENGTH_UNIT`.\n- The repo's own `testdata/models/sample_ap214.step` (`CONVERSION_BASED_UNIT('METRE', ..)`) imported 0.0647 mm wide. An inch export would import 25.4 times too small, then generate, verify and post at that size.\n\n### What changed\n\n- New `geometry/import/step_units.rs`: `mm_per_unit` reads every representation context's `GLOBAL_UNIT_ASSIGNED_CONTEXT` and resolves its `LENGTH_UNIT`:\n  - an `SI_UNIT(prefix, .METRE.)` by its prefix (`$` is the metre; KILO through NANO);\n  - a `CONVERSION_BASED_UNIT(name, #m)` by its `LENGTH_MEASURE_WITH_UNIT` value times that measure's own unit, so `'INCH'` -> 25.4 x millimetre.\n- `step::parse` reads the unit before tessellating and scales the facets to millimetres afterwards. That's safe because the tolerance schedule is relative to each shell's extent, so the mesh quality is the same in any unit.\n- Refused by name (an `ImportError::Step`): a unit this reader can't convert, and contexts that state two different length units. A file with no unit context keeps millimetres, as before.\n- Decision record: `docs/decisions/306-step-units.md`.\n\n### Tests\n\n- `crates/app/tests/import.rs`:\n  - `a_step_file_in_metres_imports_at_its_size_in_millimetres`: the AP214 sample is 60-70 mm wide.\n  - `a_step_file_in_millimetres_keeps_its_numbers`: Milling-test's max corner is unchanged.\n- `step_units::tests` (4): SI prefixes (milli, centi, none); an inch conversion through a measure is 25.4; no unit context keeps mm; two disagreeing contexts and an unknown prefix (`EXA`) refuse by name.\n- With `step.rs` and `mod.rs` checked out from `main`, `a_step_file_in_metres...` fails (\"the part is 0.06468733557556011 mm wide\"), and the millimetre pin passes either way.\n\n### What ran (Windows 10, local, built from upstream `main` 8b0d784 plus this branch)\n\n- The quick gate's plan for this diff (app lib, `large_models`, `stock_export`), plus the whole `import` suite: 92 + 22 + 3 + 2 pass, 1 ignored.\n- Every other app suite that imports a STEP fixture: `area_controls`, `area_open`, `commands_undo`, `heights_selection`, `lead_approach`, `level_links`, `pick_agnostic`, `pocket3d_hsm`, `shoulder_adaptive`, `solid_law`, `wall_arc_entry`. 97 pass, 3 ignored. `box.step` and `Milling-test.step` both declare millimetres, so their sizes don't move.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n- A note on how this was checked: the first gate run reused another worktree's build (a shared `target` folder with older file times) and compiled none of this code. I touched every source in this worktree and confirmed with `cargo test -- --list` that the new tests were built before the runs above.\n- Not run: the windowed app, Linux, macOS, and a real inch STEP export. The inch path is covered by the unit test's conversion chain, not by an exported file.\n\nChangelog: STEP import reads the file's length unit, so a part saved in inches or metres imports at its true size; a unit it can't read is refused by name (#306)\n\nFixes #306\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
